        "id": "ex_b7_s10_1",
        "questionPrompt": "A smallholder farmer in the Northern Region of Ghana cultivates maize and cowpea. When grown as sole crops, 1.0 hectare produces 4.0 metric tonnes (t/ha) of maize and 1.5 metric tonnes of cowpea. When intercropped simultaneously on the same 1.0 hectare plot, the harvested yields are 3.0 t/ha of maize and 0.9 t/ha of cowpea.\n\n(a) Calculate the Land Equivalent Ratio (LER) for this maize-cowpea intercropping enterprise.\n(b) Explain whether the farmer achieved an agronomic yield advantage by intercropping, and state the biological reason for this outcome.",
        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: State the standard formula for Land Equivalent Ratio (LER):\nLER = (Yield of Crop 1 in Intercrop / Yield of Crop 1 in Sole Crop) + (Yield of Crop 2 in Intercrop / Yield of Crop 2 in Sole Crop)\nStep 2: Substitute the empirical yield figures into the formula:\nPartial LER for Maize = 3.0 / 4.0 = 0.75\nPartial LER for Cowpea = 0.9 / 1.5 = 0.60\nCombined LER = 0.75 + 0.60 = 1.35\nStep 3: Agronomic interpretation of the calculated LER:\nAn LER of 1.35 is strictly greater than 1.0 (LER > 1.0). This demonstrates a 35% yield advantage from intercropping. It signifies that a sole-crop farmer would require 1.35 hectares of land (0.75 ha of sole maize + 0.60 ha of sole cowpea) to produce the exact same harvest that the intercropper generated from just 1.0 hectare.\nStep 4: Biological and environmental justification:\nThe yield advantage occurs due to resource complementarity and minimized interspecific niche competition: tall maize intercepts high-angle sunlight while deep-rooted cowpea intercepts low-angle light and fixes atmospheric nitrogen via root nodule Rhizobium bacteria, enriching available soil nitrogen pools.",
        "examinerTip": "Examiner Tip: (a) LER = 1.35. (b) The farmer achieved a 35% yield advantage over sole cropping due to complementary canopy solar radiation capture and biological nitrogen fixation."
