        "questionPrompt": "A Ghanaian farmer loads bags of dried cocoa beans weighing 1,200 N into a wheelbarrow. The center of gravity of the combined load and wheelbarrow bed is located 0.4 m from the wheel axle (fulcrum). The farmer lifts upward on the handles at a distance of 1.6 m from the axle.\nAssuming the empty wheelbarrow frame contributes an additional 100 N of downward weight at the same center of gravity:\n(a) Identify the class of lever and justify your answer based on component arrangement.\n(b) Calculate the minimum upward muscular effort force (E) required by the farmer to lift the handles.\n(c) Calculate the mechanical advantage (MA) provided by the wheelbarrow.",
        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: Identify the lever class and arrangement:\nIn a wheelbarrow, the wheel axle serves as the Fulcrum (F) at one end, the upward lifting force is applied at the Handles (Effort, E) at the opposite end, and the Cargo (Load, L) is situated in the middle.\nBecause the Load is between the Fulcrum and Effort (F - L - E), the wheelbarrow is a SECOND-CLASS LEVER.\nStep 2: Determine total load force:\nTotal downward resisting load (L) = 1,200 N (cocoa) + 100 N (frame) = 1,300 N.\nLoad arm distance (d_L) = 0.4 m; Effort arm distance (d_E) = 1.6 m.\nStep 3: Apply the Principle of Moments about the fulcrum:\nSum of Clockwise Moments = Sum of Anti-Clockwise Moments\nL × d_L = E × d_E\n1,300 N × 0.4 m = E × 1.6 m\n520 N·m = E × 1.6 m\nE = 520 N·m / 1.6 m = 325 N.\nStep 4: Calculate Mechanical Advantage (MA):\nMA = Load / Effort = 1,300 N / 325 N = 4.0.",
