        "questionPrompt": "During a field study along the Odaw River drainage basin in the Korle Klottey Municipal Assembly, environmental science students sampled a 500 kg composite mass of uncollected solid waste recovered from an occluded storm drain grate.\nThe sorted gravimetric analysis yielded:\n- Organic food scraps and vegetable peelings: 210 kg\n- Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) water sachets and film plastics: 135 kg\n- Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) beverage bottles: 65 kg\n- Sand, silt, and road sweepings: 50 kg\n- Metal beverage cans and scrap iron: 25 kg\n- Glass bottles and broken ceramics: 15 kg\n\n(a) Calculate the percentage mass composition of: (1) Biodegradable waste fraction; (2) Non-biodegradable synthetic plastic fraction (LDPE + PET).\n(b) Explain the fluid dynamic mechanism through which discarded plastic sachets and bottles transform an engineered 4.0 m wide rectangular concrete storm culvert with an initial volumetric flow capacity of 24.0 m³/s into a catastrophic flash flood vector during a 60 mm/hr torrential rainstorm.\n(c) Propose two immediate municipal engineering interventions and two long-term school community environmental policy actions to permanently resolve this drainage occlusion crisis.",
        "stepByStepSolution": "Step 1: Calculate gravimetric mass percentages:\nTotal sample mass = 500 kg.\n(1) Biodegradable fraction: (210 kg / 500 kg) × 100% = 42.0%.\n(2) Non-biodegradable synthetic plastic fraction: ((135 kg + 65 kg) / 500 kg) × 100% = (200 kg / 500 kg) × 100% = 40.0%.\nStep 2: Fluid dynamic mechanism of storm culvert occlusion:\nBuoyant LDPE water sachets and PET bottles float on stormwater runoff and aggregate at culvert inlets and bridge grates, forming an impermeable physical 'plastic dam'.\nSilt and sand deposit against this barrier, reducing channel cross-sectional area (A) and drastically increasing Manning's hydraulic roughness coefficient (n).\nAccording to Manning's flow equation (Q = (1/n) × A × R^(2/3) × S^(1/2)), discharge capacity Q plummets far below storm inflow, causing hydraulic backwater to overtop embankments and submerge residential areas.\nStep 3: Propose interventions:\nImmediate engineering: (1) Install automated mechanical trash rakes and upstream heavy-duty steel deflection boom screens; (2) Excavate silt-trap retention basins upstream.\nLong-term policy: (1) Mandatory source segregation in school canteens and homes using color-coded bins linked to plastic buy-back centers; (2) Enforce municipal sanitation bye-laws with spot fines for littering and enforce extended producer responsibility (EPR) on plastic manufacturers.",
        "examinerTip": "Examiner Tip: (a) Biodegradable = 42.0%, Synthetic Plastics = 40.0%. (b) Floating plastics mat together across grates, reducing cross-sectional flow area A and multiplying Manning roughness n, choking culvert discharge capacity. (c) Engineering: automated trash rakes & silt traps; Policy: color-coded source separation & municipal spot-fine enforcement."
